From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 19:01:17 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: carry an authored primitive ID as an unsigned
 user varying

Metal's [[primitive_id]] is an unsigned value the rasterizer hands to the
fragment stage. It is not a vertex output, so a Metal vertex function whose
output struct declares it is rejected outright, taking the whole MTLLibrary
with it:

  error: type 'int' is not valid for attribute 'primitive_id'
  error: invalid return type 'VertexOut' for vertex function

A pipeline reaches that shape whenever an authored primitive ID has to travel
as a varying. A geometry shader that writes gl_PrimitiveID is the usual
producer - Mesa's zink emits one for every GL primitive Vulkan lacks, and
copies the ID unconditionally because a driver that links the stages can drop
the write - and the generated point and line raster stages republish the
application's primitive ID the same way when the fragment shader reads it.
nir_opt_varyings moves such a pair to a user slot, but only while the fragment
shader takes part in the link; the generated-raster path deliberately keeps it
out, and the geometry stage the driver emulates then leaves the ID on the
built-in slot all the way into the generated vertex function.

Spell that varying as an ordinary user attribute instead. A fragment shader
that wants the rasterizer's own primitive ID reads the system value, which
nir_to_msl still spells with the Metal attribute, so the two sources stay
distinct.

The two ends of that varying are not written by the same party: the
generated raster stages republish the ID unsigned, a geometry shader stores
gl_PrimitiveID signed, and the fragment shader reads it signed. Pin the slot
with the other shapes the translator does not take from the shader: both
ends declare the unsigned scalar of the Metal value the varying stands in
for. The stores and loads are plain scalar assignments, so the bits are
unchanged.

The slots Metal owns are declared with Metal's attribute alone. An
interpolation qualifier samples a user varying; the layer and viewport
indices are values of the primitive, and a fragment input struct no longer
adds "[[flat]]" behind "[[render_target_array_index]]" or
"[[viewport_array_index]]". A user varying without a qualifier loses the
stray space in front of its semicolon.

On the OpenGL stack the missing library made every draw of a GL quad fail
pipeline creation, and nothing caches a failure: glxgears rebuilt about twelve
pipelines per frame and ran at 2.9 fps, with 314-358 host CPU-seconds per 1000
frames against 6-8 for benchmarks that avoid quads.

The new Meson test builds the ten combinations of {monolithic, pipeline
library} x {geometry shader writes the ID, fragment reads it, neither} with
static and dynamic polygon mode, and renders three triangles so the fragment
shader can prove the value and not merely the compile. The four dynamic
polygon mode cases fail with the Metal error without this change. The MSL
translator test builds a vertex shader that stores the position, primitive
ID, layer and viewport and a fragment shader that reads the last three, all
as signed integers, and checks the declarations of both structs.
